---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import BusinessDaysCalculator from '@/components/tools/datetime/BusinessDaysCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="business-days-calculator">
  <BusinessDaysCalculator client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to count working days</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Enter the start and end dates. The count includes both when they are working days.</li>
      <li>
        Tick the days that are non-working where you are. Saturday and Sunday are pre-selected; a Friday–Saturday
        weekend or a single rest day takes one click.
      </li>
      <li>
        Add your holidays. Type a date and a name, or import a ready-made set for a year and then delete the days your
        organisation works.
      </li>
      <li>Read the working-day total and the full list of removed days, with the reason for each.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>How the count is built up</h2>
    <p>
      The calculation has three passes, and the result panel shows each one, so you can audit the number:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Calendar days: each day in the inclusive range.</li>
      <li>Minus weekend days: the weekdays you marked as non-working.</li>
      <li>
        Minus holidays: only those on a day that is still in the count after the weekend pass, so a holiday on a
        Sunday is not subtracted twice.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Spreadsheet counts often get this last step wrong when people subtract a holiday count by hand. For the same
      reason, the business days calculator lists the holidays it removed, not only a number.
    </p>

    <h2>Weekends are not Saturday and Sunday everywhere</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Region</th>
            <th>Usual weekend</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Most of Europe, the Americas, East Asia, Australia</td>
            <td>Saturday and Sunday</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>UAE (since 2022), Israel</td>
            <td>Friday and Saturday, with a half day on Friday in some sectors</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Saudi Arabia, Qatar, Kuwait, Oman, Egypt, Jordan</td>
            <td>Friday and Saturday</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Iran</td>
            <td>Thursday and Friday, or Friday only</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Nepal</td>
            <td>Saturday only</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Set the checkboxes to the working week of the contract or team you count for, which may differ from your own
      calendar.
    </p>

    <h2>Your holiday list stays on this device</h2>
    <p>
      Holidays are saved to the local storage of this browser. They are not uploaded, they stay between visits, and
      the page uses them again the next time you open it. Storage is per browser and per device, so the list does not
      follow you to your phone. Export it as CSV for a copy. Clearing site data removes it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The import buttons generate holidays from their rules, not from a fixed table, so they work for any year you
      type. They are a starting point and not legal advice: substitute days, regional holidays and company shutdowns
      vary, so review the list before you rely on the count.
    </p>

    <h2>Where working-day counts matter</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Service level agreements: "resolved within 5 business days" needs an agreed holiday calendar.</li>
      <li>Payroll and leave: annual leave is usually deducted in working days, not calendar days.</li>
      <li>Legal deadlines: many court and tax rules count clear business days and move a deadline forward if it falls on a holiday.</li>
      <li>Payment terms: banking days exclude weekends and bank holidays, so a transfer sent on Friday can arrive on Tuesday.</li>
      <li>Project planning: a 20-working-day task is four weeks only if no holidays fall inside it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Counting conventions to watch</h2>
    <p>
      Some rules count business days from an event and exclude the day of the event; others include it. This
      calculator counts an inclusive range (both endpoints, when they are working days), which is the most common
      reading of "between these dates". If your rule excludes the start day, subtract one working day from the
      result, or move the start date one day forward before you count.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
